class Solution {

    public int rangeSumBST(TreeNode root, int low, int high) {

        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        // Root is smaller than the range ,gor right subtree
        if (root.val < low) {
            return rangeSumBST(root.right, low, high);
        }

        // Root is larger than the range, go left subtree
        if (root.val > high) {
            return rangeSumBST(root.left, low, high);
        }

        // Root is inside the range,go left and right subtree
        return root.val
                + rangeSumBST(root.left, low, high)
                + rangeSumBST(root.right, low, high);
    }
}

//Since this is a BST, I can use the ordering property to prune unnecessary subtrees.
// If the current node is smaller than low, its entire left subtree is also too small, so I only search the right subtree.
// If the current node is greater than high, its entire right subtree is too large, so I only search the left subtree. 
// If the node is inside the range, I add its value and search both subtrees."

//Time: O(n) where n is the number of nodes in the BST
//Space: O(h) where h is the height of the BST due to recursion stack



















//🔥 The Three Cases

This is what you should remember for the interview.

Case 1 — Too small
if (root.val < low) {
    return rangeSumBST(root.right, low, high);
}

Because:

root < low

and everything on the left is even smaller.

➡️ Go right.

Case 2 — Too large
if (root.val > high) {
    return rangeSumBST(root.left, low, high);
}

Because:

root > high

and everything on the right is even larger.

➡️ Go left.

Case 3 — Inside range
return root.val
        + rangeSumBST(root.left, low, high)
        + rangeSumBST(root.right, low, high);

The root qualifies.

➡️ Add it and check both sides.